t__(fmt, datefmt)\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int) or max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n        ...\n```\n\nFormat: need to compute record.message via super().format? super().format() sets record.message = record.getMessage() and possibly record.exc_text. To avoid mutating shared record's msg/args, we should create a copy of the record for formatting? But default handler formats record. Approach: in format(), compute message ourselves:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        if len(message) > self.max_message_chars:\n            truncated = _truncate(message, self.max_message_chars)\n            record = copy.copy(record)  # shallow copy\n            record.msg = truncated\n            record.args = ()\n            record.message = truncated  # needed? super().format sets it\n    msg = super().format(record)\n    ...\n```\n\nBut careful: `prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]` in multiline handling — with the copy, record.message is recomputed by super().format as truncated message. Using the copy works fine.\n\ncopy module — is `copy` allowed? Allowed imports list is only for my action code, not for the repo source. Repo source can import copy. But requirement says \"日志模块只扩展格式化器和默认初始化，可增加必要标准库导入或辅助函数\". So import copy is fine.\n\nAlternatively, avoid copy by constructing a new LogRecord via logging.makeLogRecord? copy is simpler.\n\nActually careful: shallow copy of record copies `exc_info` and `stack_info` references — fine, we keep them. We aren't modifying them.\n\nBut `super().format(record)` on the copy: formatter uses record.exc_text possibly; the copy is discarded so shared record untouched. Good.\n\nTruncation math: marker len = 15 (\"...<omitted>...\" → 3+9+3 = 15). remaining = max - 15, head = ceil(remaining/2), tail = remaining - head. For 32: remaining=17, head=9, tail=8. ✓\n\ndefault init: `_setup_logger()` passes max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS. Need import: from deepinfer.environ import envs. Check logger.py imports — need to read top of logger.py. I only have the formatter class and _setup_logger. Let me check lines 1-33 of logger.py. Also need to see if logger imports envs already.\n\nAlso stats_logger: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nNote that the range evidence 342-344 was _emit_logs; the consumer is _log_worker at lines ~113-124.\n\nLet's read logger.py lines 1-33 to check imports.\n\nAlso, requirement 4 says truncation only limits message body, not prefixes; using copy with msg replaced handles that. Also if max < len(marker)? min 32 > 15 fine.\n\nEdge case: record.message when msg is not str: record.getMessage() returns str via str(msg) % args. If args empty, str(msg). Fine.\n\nAlso careful: if we copy record and set record.msg = truncated, record.args = (), then record.getMessage() returns truncated (str(truncated) % () — hmm, \"%\" in truncated with empty args: msg % () where msg contains '%'? str.__mod__ with empty tuple: if msg contains a '%' specifier it raises TypeError \"not enough arguments for format string\". Real risk: truncated message containing e.g. \"100%\". Original code path: record.getMessage() → str(msg) % self.args; if args empty, `str(msg) % ()` would also raise... Actually logging's getMessage does `if self.args: msg = msg % self.args`. So with args empty, no formatting. Good — sets args=() falsy, so no re-formatting. Fine.\n\nBut also record.message gets set by super().format via record.getMessage(). Good.\n\nNow check logger.py imports and whether envs import causes circular import: deepinfer/environ.py imports os, threading, etc. — no import of logger. deepinfer/__init__ imports environ. If logge